  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Mass of the neutron is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>67493</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math>,</p><p>mass of the proton is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>67262</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>kg</mi></math>.</p><p>Proton&#39;s rest mass is less than that of a&nbsp;neutron&#39;s rest&nbsp;mass. As particle can not decay to a state of greater mass, hence&nbsp;the&nbsp;proton does not decay to neutron outside nucleus whereas the neutron can decay&nbsp;into a proton though&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#946;</mi></math>-decay.</p>",
